{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was the ruler of Tripoli from 1149 until his death in 1152. He was a member of the Anjou dynasty which ruled the County of Tripoli from 1100 to 1289. Raymond III was the son of Ponce II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Albon. He became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1142. \n\n*Election of the ruler*\nRaymond III was elected as the ruler of Tripoli by the nobles of the county. This election was likely due to the influence of the Holy Roman Emperor Frederick I who was a supporter of the Anjou dynasty. Raymond III was a member of the Anjou dynasty which was a powerful and influential family in the Holy Roman Empire. The Anjou dynasty was known for its military prowess and its ability to expand its territories through strategic alliances and marriages.\n\n*Marriage and alliances*\nRaymond III married Alys of Courtenay who was the daughter of Theodora of Alania and Alexios I Komnenos. This marriage was a strategic alliance between the County of Tripoli and the Byzantine Empire. Raymond III and Alys had at least one son named Amalric I Count of Tripoli who would later become the ruler of the County of Tripoli. \n\n*Death and succession*\nRaymond III died in 1152 and was succeeded by his son Amalric I Count of Tripoli. Amalric I would go on to rule the County of Tripoli for the next 18 years and expand its territories through strategic alliances and military campaigns. Raymond III's death marked the beginning of a new era for the County of Tripoli and the Anjou dynasty which would shape the course of its history for centuries to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0013606823049485683}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0001284993631998077}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.19242680072784424}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -3.18283746310044